        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To simplify an expression like <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -(3+b)]</span>, we need to <strong>distribute</strong> the negative sign across each of the terms inside the parentheses. This can be accomplished by treating the negative sign like multiplication by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-1).]</span> For example:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u2212 (3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1)(3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1) \u22c5 3 + (\u2212 1) \u22c5 b|=; \u2212 3 + (\u2212 b)|=; \u2212 3 \u2212 b]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "To simplify an expression like [MATH: -(3+b)], we need to distribute the negative sign across each of the terms inside the parentheses. This can be accomplished by treating the negative sign like multiplication by [MATH: (-1).] For example:\n[MATH: \u2212 (3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1)(3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1) \u22c5 3 + (\u2212 1) \u22c5 b|=; \u2212 3 + (\u2212 b)|=; \u2212 3 \u2212 b]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In these cases, the negative sign in front of the parentheses represents the <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -1]</span>. However, to save space, only the negative sign is shown.</p>",
            "readable_text": "In these cases, the negative sign in front of the parentheses represents the [MATH: -1]. However, to save space, only the negative sign is shown.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo simplify an expression like [MATH: -(3+b)], we need to distribute the negative sign across each of the terms inside the parentheses. This can be accomplished by treating the negative sign like multiplication by [MATH: (-1).] For example:\n[MATH: \u2212 (3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1)(3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1) \u22c5 3 + (\u2212 1) \u22c5 b|=; \u2212 3 + (\u2212 b)|=; \u2212 3 \u2212 b]\n\nContent:\nIn these cases, the negative sign in front of the parentheses represents the [MATH: -1]. However, to save space, only the negative sign is shown."
      },
