    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo simplify an expression like [MATH: -(3+b)], we need to distribute the negative sign across each of the terms inside the parentheses. This can be accomplished by treating the negative sign like multiplication by [MATH: (-1).] For example:\n[MATH: \u2212 (3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1)(3 + b)|=; (\u2212 1) \u22c5 3 + (\u2212 1) \u22c5 b|=; \u2212 3 + (\u2212 b)|=; \u2212 3 \u2212 b]\n\nContent:\nIn these cases, the negative sign in front of the parentheses represents the [MATH: -1]. However, to save space, only the negative sign is shown.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Distributing the Negative Sign over the Sum of Two Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: - \\left( 3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we rewrite the negative sign as multiplication by [MATH: (-1)\\mathbin:]\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n- \\left( 3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right)&=\\\n(-1) \\left( 3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right)\n}]\n\nThen, we distribute the [MATH: (-1)] to each term in the parentheses, and simplify:\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n(-1) \\left( 3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right)&=\\\n(-1) (3n)+(-1) \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right)&=\\\n-3n+ \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right) &=\\\n-3n-\\dfrac{1}{2}k\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nExpand [MATH: \u2212 (y + 7z).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: y \u2212 7z]\nb. [MATH: y + 7z]\nc. [MATH: 7y + z]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 y + 7z]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 y \u2212 7z]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nExpand [MATH: \u2212 ((1)/(2) + 3c).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3 + (1)/(2)c]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(2) + 3c]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(2) \u2212 3c]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(2) + 3c]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(2) \u2212 3c]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Distributing the Negative Sign over the Difference of Two Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: -(2x-1.3).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we rewrite the negative sign as multiplication by [MATH: (-1)\\mathbin:]\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n-(2x-1.3)&=\\\n(-1)(2x-1.3)\n}]\n\nThen, we distribute the [MATH: (-1)] to each term in the parentheses, and simplify:\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n(-1)(2x-1.3)&=\\\n(-1) (2x)-(-1) (1.3)&=\\\n-2x-(-1.3)&=\\\n-2x+1.3\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \u2212 (0.3x \u2212 0.2y).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 0.3x \u2212 0.2y]\nb. [MATH: 0.3x + 0.2y]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 0.3x + 0.2y]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 0.2x \u2212 0.4y]\ne. [MATH: 0.3x \u2212 0.2y]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \u2212 (a \u2212 2b).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2a + b]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 a + 2b]\nc. [MATH: a \u2212 b]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 a \u2212 2b]\ne. [MATH: a + 2b]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Tutorial: Rule of Thumb\n\nContent:\nBy distributing the negative sign across each of the terms inside the parentheses, we're merely changing the sign of each term. In other words:\n\nContent:\n- If a term was positive inside the parentheses, it becomes negative.\n- If a term was negative inside the parentheses, it becomes positive.\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Example: Distributing the Negative over More Than Two Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: -\\left( -p + 2q - \\dfrac{1}{3} \\right).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo distribute the negative sign to the terms inside the parentheses, all we have to do is change the sign of each term.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 (\u2212 p + 2q \u2212 (1)/(3))|=; p \u2212 2q + (1)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \u2212 (10j \u2212 11k + 15m).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 10j + 11k \u2212 15m]\nb. [MATH: 10j + 11k + 15m]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 10j + 11k \u2212 15m]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 10j \u2212 11k \u2212 15m]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 10j + 11k + 15m]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \u2212 (\u2212 a + b \u2212 c).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: a \u2212 b \u2212 c]\nb. [MATH: a \u2212 b + c]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 a \u2212 b \u2212 c]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 a + b \u2212 c]\ne. [MATH: a + b + c]"
  }
